From: ane-stack
Subject: [PATCH 0008] test: hardware-conditional test discipline (TEST-2)

A hardware-gated test that runs without hardware had three honest options and
one dishonest one. The dishonest one — what the suite did at 27 call sites —
is `return` early, so cargo reports the test as PASSED while it did no work.
On a CI runner with no Apple hardware, the entire e2e surface was a wall of
green that meant nothing.

This patch routes every runtime hardware gate through a shared helper,
`mil_compile::hw_gate(reason, available)`:

- default (hardware absent): prints a loud, greppable `SKIP: hardware
  unavailable (<reason>)` line and returns — the skip is now visible and
  machine-countable instead of silent.
- `MIL_REQUIRE_HW=1`: the skip becomes a hard failure, so CI can assert
  hardware coverage rather than silently losing it.

Also adds `mil_compile::count_hw_skips(output)` for a CI summary step to turn
"0 failed" into "N passed, M skipped" honestly.

Shapes converted (27 gates across 10 files): the `if !coremlc() { return; }`,
`if mil_compile::coremlc_path().is_none() { return; }`, the ops_e2e
return-None helper, and the lora_e2e compound `!hf.exists() || ...` gate.
Tests already `#[ignore]`d keep their attribute (cargo counts those correctly
as "ignored"); this fixes the runtime-skip lie.

Verified on a no-hardware runner (shadowed xcrun): each gated test emits a
SKIP line and the run passes with 0 failures; with MIL_REQUIRE_HW=1 the same
run fails loudly. Full suite green on hardware.

Stacking: apply after 0001..0007.
